In this lesson, we'll look at angles between lines and planes in three dimensions. The big idea is simple: when a 3D angle feels tricky, drop a perpendicular and it becomes a flat, 2D problem you already know how to solve. First, the angle between two lines. Just slide one line along without rotating it until it meets the other. The angle at that meeting point is the angle between them. Next, the angle between a line and a plane. Pick any point on the line and drop a straight-down perpendicular onto the plane. The angle between the line and its own shadow, that projection, is the angle between the line and the plane. Why bother? Because this trick pops up everywhere, from surveying land and setting roof pitch to figuring out ramp gradients and 3D navigation. Once you've drawn that perpendicular, your old Pythagoras and trigonometry toolkit takes over inside the right triangle you've created. Now let's see it in action.